WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:09.809
SIERRA: Japan. A place known for its exceptionally
good food, its pop culture, and its sprawling,

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:14.960
fast-paced cities. When it comes to geopolitics
though, it doesn t get talked about as much

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:20.430
as neighbors like Russia and China. But out
of the spotlight, and behind the curtain,

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:26.689
Japan is a powerful global player, and one
of the United States most important allies.

00:00:26.689 --> 00:00:32.640
The partnership has been cemented over decades,
grounded in shared goals, and shared values.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:39.550
But Japan also has a problem: it s running
out of people. Its population is growing older,

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:44.440
and not enough babies are being born. If the
trend continues, it could weaken the country

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:49.690
s role on the world stage, and this could
have serious implications for the U.S., and

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:56.390
the future of Asia. I m Gabrielle Sierra,
and this is Why It Matters, today, a demographic

00:00:56.390 --> 00:01:02.010
problem in Japan.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:11.310
SIERRA: So the world's attention is going
to be turning to Japan soon because the Olympic

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:16.250
Games are going to be beginning. Is there
a simple way to describe what life is like

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:17.250
there?

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:22.549
RICH: It's sort of the lifestyle superpower
of the world. It's a very easy place to live,

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:24.390
at least on the surface.

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:30.120
SIERRA: This is Motoko Rich. She s the Tokyo
bureau chief for the New York Times.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:34.729
RICH: And then I think it's when you dig just
a little bit below, you see that there are

00:01:34.729 --> 00:01:39.180
a lot of problems, some of them looming in
the future and some of them very much there

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:44.680
today, and that makes it a place of somewhat
cognitive dissonance, maybe like all societies,

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:50.080
but I think Japan, because it looks so beautiful
and so efficient and so fun, especially in

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:55.219
a place like Tokyo or Osaka and Kyoto on the
surface, I think people don't realize what

00:01:55.219 --> 00:01:56.240
might be lurking beneath.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:01.070
SIERRA: Right. So let's talk about one of
those issues. Can you tell me a little bit

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:03.469
about Japan's demographic problems?

00:02:03.469 --> 00:02:09.450
RICH: Well, the predominant issue is that
it is rapidly aging. It's currently considered

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:13.100
the oldest country in the world in terms of
the proportion of the population that's already

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:20.040
over 65. It's approaching about 30%, and it
will be more than that in about 10 years.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:24.180
And the population has also been declining
for the last decade. So there are a lot of

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:27.840
old people and fewer and fewer young people.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:33.580
SIERRA: It's just actually kind of hard for
me to imagine life in a country where everyone

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:39.580
is so old. I was kind of hoping that you could
help me imagine it. So let's say I'm a young

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:45.040
woman in Japan, maybe in the countryside or
maybe in a city, can you take me sort of on

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:49.680
a walk? Like what do I see? Do I feel this
happening around me?

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:53.010
RICH: Well, if you're a young woman in the
countryside, you're alone.

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:54.010
SIERRA: Wow.

00:02:54.010 --> 00:03:00.230
RICH: I mean very recently I reported about
a young woman who she and her sister were

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:06.769
literally the last people to be born in their
town. In their late 20s, they're the youngest

00:03:06.769 --> 00:03:12.459
people in their town. And she left her town
to study to become a nurse about two hours

00:03:12.459 --> 00:03:16.900
away, but she's planning to go back and she's
going to specialize in geriatrics because

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:21.640
what else is there to do? She can't be a pediatric
nurse or a neonatal nurse.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:22.640
SIERRA: Right.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:28.290
RICH: If she were to walk to the bank, there
will be a rack with three different sets of

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:29.640
prescription reading glasses.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:30.640
SIERRA: Oh my gosh.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.971
RICH: Because the assumption is that somebody
might walk in, forget their glasses, and need

00:03:33.971 --> 00:03:38.890
the reading glasses to look at the deposit
slip. If you go into the city office and there

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:44.099
are seats at the counter, there will be little
slots to put your cane. And then, like I said,

00:03:44.099 --> 00:03:51.590
walking around town, everybody will be old.
With fewer babies being born and a rapidly

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:58.459
aging population, Japan is facing an unprecedented
demographic crisis with vast social, economic,

00:03:58.459 --> 00:04:03.300
and political repercussions. Couples are waiting
longer to get married, are working more and

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:06.380
having fewer children, and there's a huge
cultural difference.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:09.610
SHAW: Within around 20 years it's going to
be Losing 1 million people a year; the size

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:11.769
of a city.

00:04:11.769 --> 00:04:17.259
SIERRA: This slow down may come as a surprise
if you associate Japan with bustling cities

00:04:17.259 --> 00:04:24.070
and cutting-edge youth culture. But it s real.
Japan s 2020 census recorded a population

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:31.290
decline of 800,000 people, a year later the
percentage of its population under age 14

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:38.100
had fallen to its lowest level ever - just
11 percent. It s a big change. For much of

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:44.120
the 20th century, Japan was a story of booming
growth. By the 1980s, it had become the world

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:48.820
s second-strongest economy, and there were
widespread fears that it might even displace

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:54.139
the United States. That didn t happen, but
the country remains a pillar of the global

00:04:54.139 --> 00:05:00.070
economy and a considerable force in international
diplomacy. But all of this is threatened by

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:02.750
their declining population.

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:08.190
SIERRA: So what kind of obstacles is this
issue creating, then, for Japan? What are

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:10.830
people worried about at the national level?

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:14.980
SMITH: So first and foremost, obviously, is
that the government will have to spend more

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:21.880
to take care of the elderly in Japan, be that
in health care costs, or be it in pensions.

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:26.790
SIERRA: This is Sheila Smith, Senior Fellow
for Asia Pacific studies at the Council on

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:30.870
Foreign Relations, and a widely followed expert
on Japan.

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:36.030
SMITH: The second is and it's related also
to the government, its revenue and that is

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:40.220
that the working population, as it gets smaller
right as more people retire and then the population

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:45.380
doesn't grow the share of the people who are
working age diminishes and that means less

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:51.530
revenue, less taxes, less income taxes specifically.
So there'll be fewer Japanese taking care

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:56.460
of a larger proportion of the population.
So that ratio between working and retiree

00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:01.280
will shift considerably. But there are larger
social questions and Japan for the last decade

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:06.560
or more has been grappling with some of this.
Traditionally, the Japanese lived with their

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:11.810
nuclear family, so often you'd have grandma
and grandpa living in the house with you.

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:17.810
That has changed as Japan became more urbanized
as attitudes towards households and families

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:23.280
changed. You've now got a lot of elderly people
living alone. And so you've had a pretty widespread

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:28.990
shift in the norms regarding elderly care.
So now you have a lot more nursing facilities,

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:33.760
you've got public private partnerships on
how to deal with that, especially as I noted

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:40.460
in rural sectors of Japan, this elderly care
question doesn't just affect, you know, do

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:45.130
we build a home? And how do we get caregivers
to take care of elderly people, but also just

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:49.449
simply the hollowing out of Japan's smaller
cities and towns.

00:06:49.449 --> 00:06:55.750
SIERRA: Eldercare is expensive, and once they
retire, older people are no longer contributing

00:06:55.750 --> 00:07:02.030
directly to the economy through labor. Countries
need young and working age people to pay taxes

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:07.229
that keep the country running, and to start
the businesses that fuel growth. If the percentage

00:07:07.229 --> 00:07:12.000
of elderly keeps rising, and the percentage
of young people keeps falling, this system

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:19.000
breaks down, tax revenue decreases, and labor
shortages set in. One UN study found that

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:25.060
Japan would have to raise the retirement age
to 77 in order to maintain its current worker-to-retiree

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:26.210
ratio.

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:31.590
RICH: Japan is kind of a place where the world
can look to see what the future looks like,

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:35.350
right? We now know that the population is
declining in the United States. China's going

00:07:35.350 --> 00:07:39.259
to be facing this. South Korea is already
facing this. So there are a lot of lessons

00:07:39.259 --> 00:07:43.259
that could be learned by looking at Japan.
A little bit of history. We go back to after

00:07:43.259 --> 00:07:50.060
the war when Japan was occupied by the United
States until early 1950s, and then after that,

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:54.349
it kind of took off economically. And the
way they accomplished that, by and large,

00:07:54.349 --> 00:07:58.850
was creating this work culture where everyone
sort of were pulling together as part of what

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:04.470
they call Japan Inc to kind of rebuild the
country and make it an economic superpower.

00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:10.940
And it was largely men, and the men would
devote their entire lives to the company,

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:16.280
not just in terms of their entire careers,
but literally every day of their lives, that

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:21.509
they were there long, long hours. They often
worked weekends. And the structure that enabled

00:08:21.509 --> 00:08:27.680
that was having a very strong traditional
nuclear family with women at home taking care

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:32.560
of the children. Then the bust came when the
kind of real estate bubble in Japan collapsed

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:37.870
in the very early '90s, and then women were
starting to go back to work partly because

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:42.800
of necessity. If they were part of a nuclear
family, the family needed more income, the

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:46.360
women were entering the workforce. Then there
was this sense, a little bit of, trying to

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:51.350
empower women and actually allow women and
encourage women to have their own careers.

00:08:51.350 --> 00:08:57.170
And then at the same time, Japan was not kind
of changing those standards of what was expected

00:08:57.170 --> 00:09:02.620
of women in the home. So if they wanted to
have those careers but also marry and have

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:07.420
children, they were expected to do so much
at home, that many women were looking at that

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:12.160
and saying, "We can't do that. We can't do
both." I mean, these are similar themes that

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:17.040
are now just starting to play out in the United
States, but Japan has been kind of ahead of

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:21.120
the curve, if you will, on that trend since
the '90s.

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:27.160
SIERRA: Many women are choosing not to marry,
and to delay or avoid childbirth altogether.

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:32.010
As a result, the youthful side of Japan s
population scale grows lighter and lighter.

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:37.150
Meanwhile, the elderly side grows heavier.
And that s because Japanese people tend to

00:09:37.150 --> 00:09:43.800
live for a very long time. The average life
expectancy in Japan is 84 and climbing, the

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:46.779
highest of any nation on earth.

00:09:46.779 --> 00:09:52.209
SMITH: So every year in Japan, when you're
watching TV, you get to see, you know, the

00:09:52.209 --> 00:09:58.820
elderly ladies that are 104, 108, or 106,
you know, so their diet has allowed them to

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:03.170
have a healthy, older age. But that means
that the percentage of the population that's

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:06.580
elderly is larger because of that extended
life expectancy.

00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:12.420
RICH: Longevity is of a feature of Japan.
People live really long lives, they're very

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:16.950
healthy, and you can just sort of see that
when you walk around. I mean, we use the word

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:21.980
genki, which means very healthy, and it's
sort of astonishing I think, certainly compared

00:10:21.980 --> 00:10:28.329
to the West, where you just see people hiking
and walking, and even if they have canes,

00:10:28.329 --> 00:10:32.640
even old women who are stooped over and practically
horizontal with the ground are still doing

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:38.820
their own shopping. It just sort of extraordinary
how people continue to be active at such old

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:40.329
ages in Japan.

00:10:40.329 --> 00:10:47.120
SIERRA: Can you just paint a picture of what
Japan might face if its demographic decline

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:49.709
continues? You know, what are the fears?

00:10:49.709 --> 00:10:55.230
SMITH: So I think the fears of an aging society
are obviously that Japan will lose its economic

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:59.760
vitality. And therefore it won't be able to
compete internationally in the way it has

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:05.421
so demonstrably in the post war era. But there's
also the Japanese fear, of course, that their

00:11:05.421 --> 00:11:09.611
aging society may compromise your ability
to manage the geostrategic changes that are

00:11:09.611 --> 00:11:14.970
taking place in the region. China is on the
rise. It's a bigger, more populous, more economically

00:11:14.970 --> 00:11:20.570
vibrant country, Japan is going to have to
maintain its own economic and political vibrancy

00:11:20.570 --> 00:11:25.540
if it is to live in the world, which has a
stronger China. Finally, I think it's important

00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:32.130
that Japan doesn't turn inwards, that younger
Japanese don't say that the problems of the

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:36.850
world are too much for them. Japan has been
such a partner of the United States and many

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:41.361
other countries in support of the world that
we've created in the post war period. It's

00:11:41.361 --> 00:11:49.130
been a vibrant advocate of liberal values,
of an open trading system of peace and security.

00:11:49.130 --> 00:11:54.680
And so I think there is a slight danger that
Japan's younger population will turn inwards

00:11:54.680 --> 00:12:00.790
and Japan will no longer be the strong partner
of ours, and of Europeans and of many others

00:12:00.790 --> 00:12:05.639
in Asia, that you'll see a quiet isolated
Japan and that would be a loss for all of

00:12:05.639 --> 00:12:06.639
us.

00:12:06.639 --> 00:12:08.980
SIERRA: Japan s demographic decline is first
and foremost a problem for Japan itself. But

00:12:08.980 --> 00:12:09.980
it could also be a problem for its many allies,
including the U.S. More on that, and a look

00:12:09.980 --> 00:12:11.839
at potential solutions, after the break.

00:12:11.839 --> 00:12:16.800
SIERRA: Hey Podcast Listeners! I m here to
tell you about another podcast I think you

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:22.620
ll enjoy: The Last Archive from Harvard historian
and New Yorker writer Jill Lepore. For this

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:28.870
season, Lepore is investigating purveyors
of doubt hoaxers, fraudsters, pseudoscientists

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:33.981
and how the peddling of doubt evolved over
the 20th century, leading all the way to the

00:12:33.981 --> 00:12:40.649
Capitol Insurrection on January 6, 2021. This
show is unlike anything you ve heard before:

00:12:40.649 --> 00:12:46.490
history animated by long lost archival tapes
and documents, intrepid reporting, and radio

00:12:46.490 --> 00:12:53.450
drama reenactments. The Last Archive unfurls
like a classic 1930s gumshoe mystery but takes

00:12:53.450 --> 00:12:59.910
on the big issues of today. How do we know
what we know, and why does it seem like lately

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:04.940
as if we don t know anything at all. Listen
to The Last Archive wherever you get your

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:06.810
podcasts. From Pushkin Industries.

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:18.279
Joe BIDEN: Prime Minister Suga and I affirmed
our ironclad support for U.S.-Japanese alliance,

00:13:18.279 --> 00:13:26.010
and for our shared security. We are committed
to working together to take on the challenges

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:31.160
from China and on issues like the East China
Sea and the South China Sea. Our relationship

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:38.279
is in full bloom because the Japanese and
American people understand more and more that

00:13:38.279 --> 00:13:43.570
our two nations and other like-minded liberal
democracies must work together.

00:13:43.570 --> 00:13:49.070
SIERRA: If you went back in time 75 years,
a lot of Americans would be surprised to hear

00:13:49.070 --> 00:13:54.829
that the U.S. and Japan are now so close.
After all, the two countries were fierce adversaries

00:13:54.829 --> 00:14:00.670
in World War II. But in the wake of that conflict,
they forged a partnership that has grown and

00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:05.430
thrived through the decades. The alliance
was initially designed to address Cold War-era

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:10.310
concerns in Asia. But that focus has changed.

00:14:10.310 --> 00:14:16.540
SIERRA: Can you sort of walk me through the
most important elements of the US Japan alliance

00:14:16.540 --> 00:14:18.220
as it stands now?

00:14:18.220 --> 00:14:23.329
COOPER: The US-Japan alliance is very focused
right now, on the China challenge.

00:14:23.329 --> 00:14:29.420
SIERRA: This is Zack Cooper, research fellow
at the American Enterprise Institute and Co-Director

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:34.889
of the Alliance for Securing Democracy at
the German Marshall Fund of the United States.

00:14:34.889 --> 00:14:39.949
COOPER: It's arguably the most important thing
for American leaders today, especially on

00:14:39.949 --> 00:14:45.970
foreign policy, it has been the most important
thing to Japan for years. And so in a lot

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:51.209
of ways, the Alliance is actually getting
stronger, because the challenges that we're

00:14:51.209 --> 00:14:57.800
facing are growing more severe. China is very
close to Japan, right? They share some disputed

00:14:57.800 --> 00:15:05.260
territory and maritime areas. It is the world's
second largest military and growing very rapidly.

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:11.259
It is surging that military often into areas
that are disputed with Japan, and it often

00:15:11.259 --> 00:15:18.440
exhibits some public anger at Japan. And so
I think from a Japanese view, the perceived

00:15:18.440 --> 00:15:23.529
threat level from China is quite high at the
moment. And obviously, the same is true of

00:15:23.529 --> 00:15:27.269
the United States, especially over the last
two or three years.

00:15:27.269 --> 00:15:33.509
SMITH: They have a sovereignty dispute. China
disputes Japan's administrative control over

00:15:33.509 --> 00:15:39.640
a small set of uninhabited islands in the
East China Sea. The Hong Kong security law,

00:15:39.640 --> 00:15:44.570
and the quelching of democratic practice in
Hong Kong has really worried the Japanese

00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:49.180
as well. And then of course, there's the military
activities that the Chinese have been conducting

00:15:49.180 --> 00:15:54.430
recently in and around Taiwan, which verge
on two pieces of that puzzle. One is the question

00:15:54.430 --> 00:15:58.730
about whether or not China would actually
use force against a democratic nation. And

00:15:58.730 --> 00:16:03.670
the second is, this is perilously close to
Japanese territory. So it spills over into

00:16:03.670 --> 00:16:08.550
Japanese thinking about what the Chinese might
do, or whether a conflict over Taiwan might

00:16:08.550 --> 00:16:11.639
actually spill over to involve Japan directly.

00:16:11.639 --> 00:16:17.000
SIERRA: And China isn t the only worry. Japan
is also a neighbor of North Korea, and plays

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:22.470
an essential role in U.S. military planning
for a crisis on the Korean peninsula. Plans

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:28.730
like these are only possible because Japan
hosts several U.S. military bases on its territory.

00:16:28.730 --> 00:16:36.009
COOPER: The first thing to be aware of is
that most of these bases are absolutely critical

00:16:36.009 --> 00:16:41.930
to the US military's ability to carry out
its operations in East Asia. So we are more

00:16:41.930 --> 00:16:49.079
dependent on Japan than any other location,
arguably, in the world. There are about 50,000

00:16:49.079 --> 00:16:54.220
American forces in Japan, and many of them
are accompanied. So there's something like

00:16:54.220 --> 00:16:58.160
100,000 American service members and families.

00:16:58.160 --> 00:17:03.790
RICH: Part of the reason that the United States
has its largest contingent of military personnel

00:17:03.790 --> 00:17:08.800
outside of the United States stationed in
Japan is precisely because they recognize

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:13.470
that that is a strategic location to protect
the whole region. It's not just about protecting

00:17:13.470 --> 00:17:16.890
Japan. And I went down to Okinawa one time
and was meeting with the general who's in

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:22.180
charge of the Okinawa operations where there
are 25,000 US Marines based. And he pulled

00:17:22.180 --> 00:17:27.260
down this huge wall map and just showed me,
"Look, Okinawa, Southern end of Japan. It's

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:33.429
so close to Taiwan, so close to China, it's
so strategic in the region." That is why the

00:17:33.429 --> 00:17:36.850
United States is there. If they pulled back
and everything was based in California, it

00:17:36.850 --> 00:17:41.660
would take so much longer to get to the region
in the case of the necessity to bring an aircraft

00:17:41.660 --> 00:17:47.460
carrier. They have an aircraft carrier base
in Yokosuka, Japan. So it's right here if

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:52.030
God forbid there was something that they needed
to do, they're here. And that's not just for

00:17:52.030 --> 00:17:56.030
the protection of Japan, that's for the protection
of the whole region. And in that sense, it

00:17:56.030 --> 00:17:58.680
is also for the production of the United States.

00:17:58.680 --> 00:18:04.780
SIERRA: Nobody wants war, or conflict. But
if conflict does break out in the Pacific,

00:18:04.780 --> 00:18:11.240
the U.S. is deeply dependent on Japan to be
able to face the threat. And Japan, in turn,

00:18:11.240 --> 00:18:16.960
depends on our backing. This two way street
isn t limited to military concerns. The Japanese

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:21.520
and American economies have been similarly
intertwined for a long time.

00:18:21.520 --> 00:18:26.610
COOPER: So many people don't remember this.
But Japan is the world's third largest economy

00:18:26.610 --> 00:18:32.900
and by quite a bit. So when we talk about
Japan, it still has a remarkably dynamic economy.

00:18:32.900 --> 00:18:41.169
And it is globally integrated right into supply
chains and everything from microchips to automobiles.

00:18:41.169 --> 00:18:47.940
And so Japan in a lot of ways, is a real engine
for global growth. And so sometimes I think

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:52.590
it's easy to forget that if you're buying
an automobile in the United States, there's

00:18:52.590 --> 00:18:58.520
a pretty good chance that either the car you're
buying was designed in Japan, or that a lot

00:18:58.520 --> 00:19:03.660
of its components were designed or made there.
And that's, that's going to continue, right.

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:08.950
And so I think these deep economic ties are
part of why the two countries are so closely

00:19:08.950 --> 00:19:09.950
interlinked.

00:19:09.950 --> 00:19:15.330
SMITH: Japan hovers at the number one or number
two position in terms of foreign direct investment

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:20.549
in the United States. Japan creates pretty
much around a million jobs, American jobs

00:19:20.549 --> 00:19:24.840
every year through that foreign direct investment,
in addition to providing capital and obviously

00:19:24.840 --> 00:19:29.480
goods that we Americans like to buy that are
produced here in the United States. So it's

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:33.880
a very vibrant relationship, both on the economic
side and the strategic side as well.

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:39.940
SIERRA: And there s another element at play,
too. The U.S.-Japan alliance is built in part

00:19:39.940 --> 00:19:43.900
on shared democratic values, and shared diplomatic
goals.

00:19:43.900 --> 00:19:50.590
COOPER: Japan is a democracy, it shares a
lot of the same values as the United States.

00:19:50.590 --> 00:19:57.400
And so in each of these four areas, geo strategy,
economics, technology and governance, Japan

00:19:57.400 --> 00:20:02.350
is a key player. And in fact, Japan might
be the only country in the world that shares

00:20:02.350 --> 00:20:08.679
interests along all four of those areas. I
think what's not talked about enough, though,

00:20:08.679 --> 00:20:15.290
is the fact that Japan is very active outside
of Northeast Asia. And it is seen incredibly

00:20:15.290 --> 00:20:21.800
positively, in particular, in Southeast Asia.
So these are the large and rapidly growing

00:20:21.800 --> 00:20:29.000
countries like Indonesia and Vietnam, that
are vital, not just right now, but to the

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:36.070
economy and the security situation in Asia,
say 10 or 20 years from now, in those countries,

00:20:36.070 --> 00:20:40.900
Japan has by far the highest levels of trust
of any nation, far higher than the United

00:20:40.900 --> 00:20:46.010
States, far higher than China, or even Europe.
Because Japan has been there and been present

00:20:46.010 --> 00:20:50.910
and active and helping those economies grow.
So our interests are very much aligned. We

00:20:50.910 --> 00:20:57.230
want Southeast Asian countries to be succeeding,
we want them to be democratic, protecting

00:20:57.230 --> 00:21:03.621
human rights. And Japan has very similar interests
there. So I would say across the region, we

00:21:03.621 --> 00:21:09.460
see very, very similar parallel efforts from
both Washington and Tokyo. And there are a

00:21:09.460 --> 00:21:15.049
lot of times when actually, Tokyo has more
leverage to be helpful in Asia than Washington

00:21:15.049 --> 00:21:18.549
does, because it's built up so much goodwill
over the years.

00:21:18.549 --> 00:21:22.960
SIERRA: So it's kind of like we have a good
friend in Asia, who has many good friends

00:21:22.960 --> 00:21:23.960
in Asia.

00:21:23.960 --> 00:21:25.500
COOPER: That's right.

00:21:25.500 --> 00:21:30.730
SIERRA: Japan needs a strong friend in the
U.S., and we need a strong Japan in turn.

00:21:30.730 --> 00:21:35.980
And that s where Japan s demographic problem
becomes a global problem. If Japan continues

00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:41.460
to shrink, it could begin to lose its regional
influence. It s voice could grow quieter.

00:21:41.460 --> 00:21:46.070
And this in turn could reduce the power of
the alliance. That said, Japan has explored

00:21:46.070 --> 00:21:48.900
a number of potential solutions to the problem.

00:21:48.900 --> 00:21:54.140
SIERRA: Yeah. So we know that the Japanese
government is worried about all of this for

00:21:54.140 --> 00:21:58.720
a number of reasons. So what type of solutions
are they attempting?

00:21:58.720 --> 00:22:05.740
RICH: So one of the big solutions that they
started doing under the Shinzo Abe administration,

00:22:05.740 --> 00:22:12.970
he had this, in consultation with a very high-powered
banker at Goldman Sachs, she had looked at

00:22:12.970 --> 00:22:18.140
the sort of data and said, look, you have
got this very amazing untapped resource of

00:22:18.140 --> 00:22:24.559
highly educated women who are not working
to their full potential. Why don't you empower

00:22:24.559 --> 00:22:30.370
women to not just take, kind of part-time
or contract jobs, or menial labor, but really

00:22:30.370 --> 00:22:35.309
put them in the economy in a very strong and
powerful way? And so he seized on this idea

00:22:35.309 --> 00:22:39.920
and dubbed it womenomics. And the idea was
that if we empower women and support them

00:22:39.920 --> 00:22:46.770
in the workplace, we'll now have 50% more
of the workforce that had not been fully exploited,

00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:51.029
for lack of a better word, and in some cases
that's the truth, that that will help embolden

00:22:51.029 --> 00:22:57.909
the economy. So the main initiative was to
open more daycare centers, add more slots,

00:22:57.909 --> 00:23:02.650
get more kids into daycare, but because of
the previously mentioned, I mean, it's a demographic

00:23:02.650 --> 00:23:06.120
problem, right? To open more daycare slots,
you need more daycare teachers. And if you

00:23:06.120 --> 00:23:10.031
don't have enough young people to fulfill
those jobs at the same time that there are

00:23:10.031 --> 00:23:13.500
other women who want other jobs, but need
to send their kids to daycare, it's really

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:18.900
hard to do that. So it's taken them a really
long time. The new Prime Minister Yoshihide

00:23:18.900 --> 00:23:25.100
Suga has talked about subsidizing fertility
treatments because a lot of couples have trouble

00:23:25.100 --> 00:23:29.549
getting pregnant because they're waiting until
later to try to get pregnant, and so it's

00:23:29.549 --> 00:23:33.510
sort of the same issues that are faced in
the West, but that again seems a little bit

00:23:33.510 --> 00:23:38.490
like a band-aid. They're not really addressing
the fundamental core issue, which is that

00:23:38.490 --> 00:23:43.280
because of the way the workplace culture is,
that it demands that people work these 16

00:23:43.280 --> 00:23:47.809
hour days and on weekends and be available
at all times, that's really not conducive

00:23:47.809 --> 00:23:54.679
to family life. And if the women have to do
everything at home, that is not conducive

00:23:54.679 --> 00:24:00.230
to women really advancing in the workplace.
So it's sort of this Catch-22 that women just

00:24:00.230 --> 00:24:05.600
can't get out of this cycle that they're stuck
in, even if they want to. So it's very, very

00:24:05.600 --> 00:24:06.800
difficult for women.

00:24:06.800 --> 00:24:09.300
SIERRA:
And you certainly can't go to women and be

00:24:09.300 --> 00:24:10.300
like, "Have babies,"

00:24:10.300 --> 00:24:15.669
RICH: No, which is what they have done. There
are conservative politicians who from time

00:24:15.669 --> 00:24:19.200
to time say it's a woman's duty to go out
and have at least three children and stay

00:24:19.200 --> 00:24:24.490
home and care for them. That is not an effective
policy. So I think a lot of the things that

00:24:24.490 --> 00:24:27.649
they're trying to do are sort of these little
band-aids on the edges and they're not really

00:24:27.649 --> 00:24:29.850
addressing the fundamental core issues.

00:24:29.850 --> 00:24:35.580
SIERRA: Japan s efforts to increase fertility
haven t worked so far, but that doesn t mean

00:24:35.580 --> 00:24:41.179
that such policies can t ever work. Take Sweden,
which decades ago faced its own demographic

00:24:41.179 --> 00:24:46.270
decline. Since then, the country s generous
social security systems have been credited

00:24:46.270 --> 00:24:51.620
with encouraging gender equality and giving
women the freedom to both work and care for

00:24:51.620 --> 00:24:57.760
their children. Key features include accessible
daycare services and lenient leave programs

00:24:57.760 --> 00:25:03.240
for both mothers and fathers. Over time the
strategy helped boost the country s fertility

00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:08.740
rate, and could be a potential model for other
countries. But increasing the fertility rate

00:25:08.740 --> 00:25:12.460
is not the only option for solving a demographic
problem.

00:25:12.460 --> 00:25:19.980
RICH: So, The other big, big, big elephant
in the room is immigration. That is how population

00:25:19.980 --> 00:25:23.740
growth has been maintained in a lot of big,
large aging countries.

00:25:23.740 --> 00:25:29.460
COOPER: You know, Japan has a remarkably advanced
society. Where it's actually going to struggle

00:25:29.460 --> 00:25:35.539
to find people to do critical roles. So some
of this is things like child care and elder

00:25:35.539 --> 00:25:40.500
care, where in fact, you know, people like
home health aides are going to be really important.

00:25:40.500 --> 00:25:45.909
And there just may not be enough of a population
in Japan to serve those roles. So there are

00:25:45.909 --> 00:25:50.860
a very large number of experts in this area
from the Philippines who are well trained

00:25:50.860 --> 00:25:57.331
and very capable. And bringing in more of
those types of immigrants is a potential solution

00:25:57.331 --> 00:26:03.320
for Japan to help solve some of its challenges.
So I think, increasingly, it's going to be

00:26:03.320 --> 00:26:09.070
vital for Japan to expand its labor market
look, not just internally for ways to convince

00:26:09.070 --> 00:26:15.530
women to have more children, but also externally
to find ways to bring new people into Japanese

00:26:15.530 --> 00:26:21.909
society. This isn't easy. It's socially hard.
It's politically hard. But I think it's kind

00:26:21.909 --> 00:26:27.310
of one of the only obvious solutions that's
going to be out there as Japan society gets

00:26:27.310 --> 00:26:29.850
more grey hair over the years.

00:26:29.850 --> 00:26:31.980
SIERRA: So what about automation?

00:26:31.980 --> 00:26:38.549
COOPER: Absolutely. Automation is one excellent
opportunity we've already seen Japan had this

00:26:38.549 --> 00:26:45.190
way. And you can see that interest in automation
in, for example, the military, where Japan

00:26:45.190 --> 00:26:51.029
has been investing in ways to decrease, for
example, the number of people that are on

00:26:51.029 --> 00:26:57.299
Japanese Self Defense Force ships, by increasing
robotics and automation. So effectively, it

00:26:57.299 --> 00:27:02.840
is investing more capital to decrease the
amount of labor that it needs in these areas.

00:27:02.840 --> 00:27:07.770
And that's just a smart response, right from
an economy that has more capital than it does

00:27:07.770 --> 00:27:13.299
labor. So I think automation will make a lot
of sense in Japan. But some of the challenges

00:27:13.299 --> 00:27:18.300
are that automation won't solve all these
problems, right, eldercare, for example, which

00:27:18.300 --> 00:27:22.750
we were just talking about. That's something
that's pretty hard to just automate. And so

00:27:22.750 --> 00:27:27.299
Japan is going to have to look to a mix of
solutions. But I would expect that automation

00:27:27.299 --> 00:27:34.110
will absolutely be one of the primary means
by which Japan tries to manage this difficult

00:27:34.110 --> 00:27:35.110
time.

00:27:35.110 --> 00:27:40.480
SIERRA: There s no doubt that Japan faces
a serious challenge curbing its decline. The

00:27:40.480 --> 00:27:45.730
cultural and social changes required to do
so are daunting. But nobody should count them

00:27:45.730 --> 00:27:50.940
out. Japan has demonstrated its capacity to
innovate and overcome challenges many times

00:27:50.940 --> 00:27:54.870
in the past. There s every reason to root
for them.

00:27:54.870 --> 00:27:59.710
SMITH: I think the danger for us here in the
United States is that we forget about Japan,

00:27:59.710 --> 00:28:06.140
we're so focused on China and its rising power.
And that Chinese power often overshadows Japan,

00:28:06.140 --> 00:28:11.350
our long term ally and dynamic economy in
its own right, but also a functioning and

00:28:11.350 --> 00:28:15.210
strong democracy. This is a friendship we
shouldn't take for granted. This is a friendship

00:28:15.210 --> 00:28:18.000
we shouldn't take for granted. And I think
it's important that Americans understand the

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:25.990
strength and the depth of the Japanese partnership
with the United States.

00:28:25.990 --> 00:28:37.910
